52. Power for provincial government to make rules for protection of mechanically propelled vessels from accidents.___ (1) The Provincial Government may make rules for the protection of mechanically propelled vessels against explosion, fire, collision and other accidents.

(2) In particular and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing power, such rules may—

1Subs. by the Inland Steam-vessels (Arndt.) Act, 1951 (25 of 1951) s., 2 for “INLAND STEAM VESSELS”.
2Subs. by A. O., 1937, for “G. G. in C.” 3For notification declaring certain articles to be dangerous goods, see Gen. R. & O.
4Subs. by A. O., 1937, for “Gazette of India” 5Subs. by Act 25 of 1951, s. 2 for “inland steam-vessel”.
6Subs. ibid., for “steam-vessel” 7Subs ibid., for “inland steam-vessel”.
8Subs. by the Inland Steam-vessels (Amdt.) Act, 1951 (25 of 1951), s. 2, for “steam-vessel”.
9Subs. by A. O., 1937, for “L. G.”.

(a) prescribe the conditions on, and subject to, which dangerous goods may be carried on board mechanically propelled vessels;
(b) prescribe precautions to be taken to prevent explosions or fires on board mechanically propelled vessels;
(c) prescribed the apparatus which is to be kept on board mechanically propelled vessels, for the purpose of extinguishing fires;
(d) regulate the making of sound signals;
(e) regulate the carriage and exhibition of lights by mechanically propelled vessels;
(f) regulate the carriage and exhibition of lights by other vessels on specified inland waters on which mechanically propelled vessels ply;
(g) prescribe the steering rules to be observed;
(h) regulate the towing of vessels astern or alongside;
(i) prescribe the speed at which mechanically propelled vessels may be navigated in specified areas; and
(j) regulate the navigation of mechanically propelled vessels to prevent danger to other vessels, or to the banks, channels, navigation marks or any property, moveable or immoveable, in or abutting on navigable channels.

(3) Any rule made under this section may contain a provision that any person committing a breach of it shall be punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to six months, or with fine which may extend to five hundred rupees, or with both.
